Probably, but this bench alone does not prove it. The shape (throughput falling as threads rise on one lock) is what lock contention looks like, and the single-thread cost of about 42 ns per call is mostly `Instant::now()` plus an uncontended lock. I would confirm with a profile before changing anything.

If it holds up, in order of effort:

- **Move the clock out of the lock.** Take `Instant::now()` before `lock()`; it is a syscall-free read on Linux but still shows up under contention.
- **Make the hot state atomic.** Pack tokens (in milli-tokens) and the last refill time into one `AtomicU64` and use a compare-exchange loop, so a rejected request never takes a lock.
- **Shard per route**, not per client, only if a single client key is genuinely this hot. Most floods hit many keys, which the `DashMap` already spreads.

```rust
// sketch of the atomic version
let mut cur = self.state.load(Ordering::Acquire);
loop {
    let (tokens, ts) = unpack(cur);
    let next = match refill_and_take(tokens, ts, now, n) {
        Some(s) => pack(s),
        None => return false,
    };
    match self.state.compare_exchange_weak(cur, next, Ordering::AcqRel, Ordering::Acquire) {
        Ok(_) => return true,
        Err(seen) => cur = seen,
    }
}
```

I would add a many-keys variant of the flood bench first, so we know whether real traffic even sees this.

